AB  - The purpose of this investigation was to determine the effect of hydraulic resistance training at either high or low velocity on peak torque and anaerobic power. Sixteen cyclists were randomly assigned to two training groups: group 1 (n = 8) trained the lower body musculature at high velocity (approximately 3.14 rad.s-1) while group 2 (n = 8) trained at low velocity (approximately 1.05 rad.s-1). Results showed that both groups improved significantly in peak torque for knee extension and  flexion on a Cybex II+ isokinetic dynamometer at all angular velocities tested. Low velocity training adaptations were more specific to the velocity of training than high velocity training. Power output during a 30 second anaerobic cycle ergometer test was significantly elevated after four weeks with no further improvement noted after eight weeks of training. These results show that peak torque adaptations to high and low velocity resistance training are similar at slow but not fast velocities and only slight improvements in anaerobic power output can be obtained with this type of training.
