AB  - Introduction: This study assessed the effect of individualized, domain-based exercise intensity prescription on changes in maximal oxygen uptake ((V) over dotO(2max)) and submaximal thresholds. Methods: Eighty-four young healthy participants (42 females, 42 males) were randomly assigned to six age, sex, and (V) over dotO(2max)-matched groups (14 participants each). Groups performed continuous cycling in the 1) moderate (MOD), 2) lower heavy (HVY1), and 3) upper heavy-intensity (HVY2) domain; interval cycling in the form of 4) high-intensity interval training (HIIT) in the severe-intensity domain, or 5) sprint-interval training (SIT) in the extreme-intensity domain; or no exercise for 6) control (CON). All training groups, except SIT, were work-matched. Training participants completed three sessions per week for 6 wk with physiological evaluations performed at PRE, MID, and POST intervention. Results: Compared with the change in (V) over dotO(2max) (triangle(V) over dotO(2max)) in CON (0.1 +/- 1.2 mL center dot kg(-1)center dot min(-1)), all training groups, except MOD (1.8 +/- 2.7 mL center dot kg(-1)center dot min(-1)), demonstrated a significant increase (P < 0.05). HIIT produced the highest increase (6.2 +/- 2.8 mL center dot kg(-1)center dot min(-1)) followed by HVY2 (5.4 +/- 2.3 mL center dot kg(-1)center dot min(-1)), SIT (4.7 +/- 2.3 mL center dot kg(-1)center dot min(-1)), and HVY1 (3.3 +/- 2.4 mL center dot kg(-1)center dot min(-1)), respectively. The Delta PO at the estimated lactate threshold (theta(LT)) was similar across HVY1, HVY2, HIIT, and SIT, which were all greater than CON (P < 0.05). The Delta(V) over dotO(2) and Delta PO at theta(LT) for MOD was not different from CON (P > 0.05). HIIT produced the highest Delta PO at maximal metabolic steady state, which was greater than CON, MOD, and SIT (P < 0.05). Conclusions: This study demonstrated that i) exercise intensity is a key component determining changes in (V) over dotO(2max) and submaximal thresholds and ii) exercise intensity domain-based prescription allows for a homogenous metabolic stimulus across individuals.
