AB  - <p style="margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px">Lactate threshold (LT) testing is fundamental for endurance performance assessment and training prescription. Although laboratory protocols remain the reference standard, field-based tests may better reflect the ecological and physiological demands of real-world exercise. Oxidative stress biomarkers may provide additional insight into the metabolic differences between these testing environments. To aim of the study was to compare the physiological and biochemical responses to standardized field and laboratory lactate threshold tests in endurance athletes and to determine whether oxidative stress indices can serve as comparative biomarkers of metabolic load. Thirty-six male endurance athletes (24.3 +/- 3.1 years; VO(2)max: 62.4 +/- 5.2 ml/kg/min) completed both a six-stage outdoor track test and a laboratory treadmill protocol in a randomized crossover design with a 72-h washout period. Blood lactate was measured during each stage to determine LT1 and LT2. LT1 (first lactate threshold) was defined as the first sustained rise in blood lactate above resting baseline (log-log method), and LT2 (second lactate threshold) was defined as the point of rapid, non-linear lactate accumulation, identified using the modified Dmax method. Venous blood samples were collected before and after exercise for malondialdehyde (MDA), superoxide dismutase (SOD), and glutathione peroxidase (GPx) analysis. Agreement between field and laboratory thresholds was assessed using intraclass correlation coefficients (ICC), Bland-Altman analysis, and Pearson correlations. Excellent agreement was observed between field and laboratory tests for LT1 and LT2 (ICC = 0.91-0.93; P < 0.001). However, despite similar threshold values, the field test produced significantly greater oxidative stress responses than the laboratory test. Post-exercise MDA, SOD, and GPx concentrations were all higher following field testing (P < 0.01). Exercise-induced changes in MDA and SOD were strongly associated with laboratory LT2 speed and VO(2)max (r = 0.74-0.79, P < 0.001), indicating that oxidative biomarkers captured physiological differences not reflected by threshold velocity alone. Field and laboratory lactate threshold tests demonstrate comparable validity but evoke distinct physiological and biochemical responses. The greater oxidative stress observed after field testing suggests that ecological exercise conditions impose a larger metabolic challenge than controlled laboratory exercise.
