KW  - ESSENTIAL-HYPERTENSION
KW  - RESISTANCE ARTERIES
KW  - ENDURANCE EXERCISE
KW  - LACTATE PRODUCTION
AB  - During aerobic exercise vascular tone is closely regulated to meet the metabolic demands of active muscle without compromising blood flow to other regions. The control systems which underlie this regulation are complex and include the local effects of vasoactive mediators, sympathetic modulation and circulating hormonal influences. Regular exercise evokes adaptations in these systems which increase cardiovascular functional capacity and reduce cardiovascular disease risk. Training reduces the contribution of the sympathetic neuroeffector mechanism to vascular tone both at rest and at any given level of submaximal exercise. A combination of reduced central activation, altered afferent feedback from baroreceptors and muscle mechano and metaboreceptors, release of nitric oxide and reduced receptor reactivity account for these effects. Renin release appears only to be modulated by relatively intense training although normalisation of this system may be seen with moderate training in cardiac failure. Training-induced improvements in metabolic efficiencies increase oxygen extraction and fat utilisation such that production of lactate and other vasodilatory metabolites are reduced at any submaximal workrate. While these adaptations contribute to improvements in functional capacity, it is unlikely that they significantly alter vascular tone at rest, except in cases where oedema is present, such as cardiac failure. However there is increasing evidence that endothelium-derived nitric oxide may influence vascular tone in the periods between exercise bouts. In animal studies, reactivity to stimuli which mediate their effects via nitric oxide is increased by training ranging in duration from one week to several months in both conduit vessels such as the aorta, skeletal muscle and the coronary circulation. Human data confirm that resting nitric oxide production is increased during the early phases of training adaptation and that endothelium dependent dilator reserve is elevated in the peripheral circulation of highly-trained athletes. In summary, training-induced adaptations in the release of and reactivity to, vasoactive substances, influences both large and small vessel tone and thus cardiovascular risk through mechanisms including reduction in blood pressure at rest and in response to exercise and stress, elevation in arterial compliance and increased coronary reserve. Other beneficial effects of exercise on cardiovascular risk are mediated via adaptation in cardiac structure and function, oxygen extraction, lipid profile, insulin sensitivity, blood coagulation and fibrinolytic activity (C) 1998, Medikal Press.
