AB  - PurposeTo investigate the kinetics of a non-linear heart rate (HR) variability parameter (DFA alpha 1) during submaximal cycling in male and female trained and highly trained cyclists in a non-fatigued state and after repeated maximal cycling bouts.MethodsN = 47 participants (17 female) completed a cycling protocol consisting of two blocks (Block1, Block2), each including two standardised submaximal cycling bouts performed prior (PRE) and post a maximal incremental test (POST), having four time points (Block1: PRE1-POST1; Block2: PRE2-POST2). DFA alpha 1, oxygen consumption (\documentclass[12pt]{minimal} \usepackage{amsmath} \usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy} \usepackage{mathrsfs} \usepackage{upgreek} \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt} \begin{document}$${\dot{ ext{V}} ext{O}}_{{2}}$$\end{document}), HR, respiratory frequency (RF) and rating of perceived exertion (RPE) were assessed during the submaximal bouts, and \documentclass[12pt]{minimal} \usepackage{amsmath} \usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy} \usepackage{mathrsfs} \usepackage{upgreek} \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt} \begin{document}$${\dot{ ext{V}} ext{O}}_{{2}}$$\end{document} peak and maximal power output (Wmax) during the maximal tests. A linear mixed effects model was used to analyse data.ResultsBlock2 showed significantly lower \documentclass[12pt]{minimal} \usepackage{amsmath} \usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy} \usepackage{mathrsfs} \usepackage{upgreek} \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt} \begin{document}$${\dot{ ext{V}} ext{O}}_{{2}}$$\end{document} peak (beta = - 2.51 mL & centerdot;kg(-)& sup1;& centerdot;min(-)& sup1;) and Wmax (beta = - 24.3 W) values. DFA alpha 1 decreased significantly from PRE1 (1.32 +/- 0.19) to POST1 (1.12 +/- 0.34) and remained suppressed at Block2 (1.14 +/- 0.31, 1.09 +/- 0.35). \documentclass[12pt]{minimal} \usepackage{amsmath} \usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy} \usepackage{mathrsfs} \usepackage{upgreek} \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt} \begin{document}$${\dot{ ext{V}} ext{O}}_{{2}}$$\end{document} (+ 2.37 mL & centerdot;kg(-)1 & centerdot;min(-)1), HR (+ 15 bpm), RF (+ 8 breaths/min) and RPE (+ 2) increased significantly in Block1 and stayed elevated in Block2. Following POST2, HR decreased (-4 bpm) and RF increased (+ 1 breaths/min) significantly. Training status significantly influenced absolute levels of DFA alpha 1, HR, and \documentclass[12pt]{minimal} \usepackage{amsmath} \usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy} \usepackage{mathrsfs} \usepackage{upgreek} \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt} \begin{document}$${\dot{ ext{V}} ext{O}}_{{2}}$$\end{document}, but did not influence RPE or RF. Sex did not significantly alter kinetics of DFA alpha 1, HR, VO2, RPE and RF.ConclusionDFA alpha 1 during submaximal exercise reflects both acute fatigue and non-recovered state in a repeated maximal test setting and may provide complementary information on autonomic and systemic demands when interpreted alongside \documentclass[12pt]{minimal} \usepackage{amsmath} \usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy} \usepackage{mathrsfs} \usepackage{upgreek} \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt} \begin{document}$${\dot{ ext{V}} ext{O}}_{{2}}$$\end{document}, HR, RF and RPE.
