AB  - Purpose: Running performance is influenced by the interaction of biomechanical and physiological factors. Miniaturized accelerometers worn by athletes can be used to quantify mechanical aspects of running and as a noninvasive tool to assess training status and progression. The aim of this study was to define and validate a method to assess running regularity and allow the estimation of an individual's oxygen uptake ((V) over dotO(2)) and/or blood lactate-[La](b)-based on data collected with accelerometers and heart rate. Methods: Male adolescent endurance athletes completed an incremental submaximal aerobic stage test where (V) over dotO(2) and [La](b) were measured. The test was terminated when [La](b) concentration at the end of the stage exceeded 4 mmol/L. Two wireless triaxial accelerometers were placed on participants' right shank and lower back throughout the test. The root mean square (RMS) and sample entropy (SampEn) were calculated for the vertical, mediolateral, and anteroposterior components of acceleration. Results: There were significant positive correlations of acceleration and entropy variables with [La](b) and (V) over dot O-2, with moderate to high coefficients (r =.43-. 87). RMS of the shank acceleration was the most highly related with both physiological variables. When the accelerometer was attached on the trunk, SampEn of the vertical acceleration had the strongest relationship with (V) over dotO(2) (r =.76, P <.01). Conclusions: The described method analyzing running complexity may allow an assessment of gait variability, which noninvasively tracks (V) over dotO(2) and/or [La](b), allowing monitoring of fatigue or training readiness for trained adolescent individuals.
