AB  - The sinoatrial (SA) and atrioventricular (AV) node of the human heart are innervated by anatomically different parasympathetic nerves. The aim of this study is to discriminate differences in the effects of these innervations on the beat-to-beat heart rate variability (HRV). The time series of P-P and P-Q intervals of an ECG is chosen as an indicator of SA resp. AV node's activity. Correlation analysis of these time series shows significant correlation. Performing the same procedure with running averaged beat-to-beat variabilities of the signals and comparing the results to surrogate data allows the conclusion that a weak but significant correlation between the parasympathetic innervation of the SA and AV node can exist in the short-time range.
